          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi : P \to X
            a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            encodes data that assigns to each path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : [0,1] \to X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi tra
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tra_\nabla(\gamma) : P_{\gamma(0)} \to P_{\gamma(1)}
          %p
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fibers
            of the bundle, such that this assignment depends well (e.g. smoothly) on the choice of path and is compatible with composition of paths.
          %p
            This assignment is called the
            %em parallel transport
            of the connection.
          %h3#the_idea_of_parallelism The idea of parallelism
          %p
            The term “parallel” comes from one of the many equivalent definitions of the notion of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ehresmann+connection"} Ehresmann connections
          %p
            In that formulation, the connection is encoded at each point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in P
            in the total space by a decomposition of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+space"} tangent space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi p
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_p P
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi p
                  %mi P
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi p
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_p P \simeq V_p \oplus H_p
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector spaces
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mi ker
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mo *
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_p = \ker \pi_*|_p
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
                of the projection map that sends vectors in the total space to vectors in base space (this part is fixed by the choice of
                = succeed ");" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo :
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : P \to X
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mi p
                      %mo ⊂
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi p
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_p \subset T_p P
                is a
                %em choice
                of complement, such that this choice varies smoothly over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                in an evident sense and is compatible with the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            The vectors in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_p
            are called
            %em vertical
            , the vectors in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_p
            are called
            %em horizontal
            \. One may think of this as defining locally in which way the base space sits horizontally in the total space, equivalently as identifying locally a “smoothly varying local trivialization” of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            More precisely, given such a choice of horizontal subspaces, there is for every path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : [0,1] \to X
            and every choice of lift
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \gamma(0) \in P
            of the start point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma(0)
            to the total space of the bundle, a
            %em unique lift
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \gamma : [0,1] \to P
            of the entire path to the total space:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mi γ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mover
                              %mi γ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mi γ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∈
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi π
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi γ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi γ
                        %mtd
                          %mi γ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∈
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\hat \gamma(0) &amp;\stackrel{\hat \gamma}{\to}&amp; \hat \gamma(1)
                  \&amp;&amp; \in &amp; P
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; &amp;&amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\pi}}
                  \\\
                  \\gamma(0) &amp;\stackrel{\gamma}{\to}&amp; \gamma(1)
                  \&amp;&amp; \in &amp; X
                  }
                  \\,
          %p
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \gamma
            is everywhere
            %em parallel
            (to
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi σ
                  %mover
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi P
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\partial_\sigma \hat \gamma)(\sigma) \in H_{\gamma(\sigma)} \subset T_{\gamma(\sigma)} P
                  \\,.
          %p
            In other words, this means that given a path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            down in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em transport
            any point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in P_{\gamma(0)}
            above its start point
            %em parallely
            (with respect to the notion of parallelism determined by
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi tra
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tra_\nabla(\gamma)(p) \in P_{\gamma(1)}
            over the endpoint.
          %h3#holonomic_equivalence_of_curves Holonomic equivalence of curves
          %p
            %strong Definition.
            Two curves with the same endpoints are
            %strong holonomically equivalent
            if for every principal
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            This definition has many variants, depending on which curves are allowed (e.g., smooth, continuously differentiable, continuous,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rough+paths"} rough paths
          %p
            For continuously differentiable paths we have the following characterization, see Theorem 2.11 in
            %a{:href => "#BischoffLee"} Bischoff–Lee
            for an exposition and further references.
          %p
            %strong Theorem.
            Suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is a continuously differentiable path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]\to M
            in a smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'(0)=0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'(1)=0
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                for every principal
                = succeed "-bundle" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+group"} semisimple structure group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                vanishes.
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^1
                endpoint-preserving homotopy of rank at most 1 (a
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+homotopy"} thin homotopy
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                to a point.
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^1
                endpoint-preserving homotopy
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
                that contracts
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                to a point such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi im
                        %mi h
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi im
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im h \subset im x
            %li
              %p
                The map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                factors through an
                = succeed "-tree" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                such that for every embeddings
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi s
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b\colon[s,t]\to T
                such that
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a(s)=b(s)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a(t)=b(t)
                we have
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi im
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mi im
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im a=im b
            %li
              %p
                There is a Lipschitz function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\colon[0,1]\to[0,\infty)
                (a
                = succeed ")" do
                  %strong height function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(0)=h(1)
                and if
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi h
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi h
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi inf
                        %mi h
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi s
                            %mo ,
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(s)=h(t)=\inf h|_{[s,t]}
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x(s)=x(t)
            %li
              %p
                The Chen path signature of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                is trivial.
            %li
              %p
                The path
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                is word-reduced in the sense of Tlas
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#Tlas"} Theorem 1
          %h3#the_categorytheoretic_perspective The category-theoretic perspective
          %p The parallel transport-assignment of fiber-homomorphisms to paths
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi γ
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi tra
                        %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (x \stackrel{\gamma}{\to} y) \mapsto ( P_x \stackrel{tra_\nabla(\gamma)}{\to} y )
          %p enjoys the following properties:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                it is invariant under
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+homotopy"} thin homotopy
                of paths;
            %li
              %p it is compatible with composition of paths and sends constant paths to identity homomorphisms;
            %li
              %p it sends smooth families of paths to compatible smooth families of homomorphisms.
          %p This may be equivalently but more succinctly be formulated as follows:
          %p
            We say
            %em diffeological groupoid
            for an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoid"} internal groupoid
            in the category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological spaces
          %p
            The smooth paths in a smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            naturally form the diffeological groupoid called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+groupoid"} path groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-classes" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+homotopy"} thin homotopy
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            any
            = succeed "-bundle," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi At
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} At(P)
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Atiyah+Lie+groupoid"} Atiyah Lie groupoid
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsors
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fibers
            over these points.
          %p
            Then the above properties of parallel transport are equivalent to saying that we have an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+functor"} internal functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi tra
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi At
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  tra : P_1(X) \to At(P)
          %p
            that is the identity on objects. Moreover, this functor
            %em uniquely
            characterizes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            that it comes from. This means that we may identify connections on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            with their parallel transport functors.
          %p
            But even the bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            itself is encoded in such functors. If instead of looking at the category of internal groupoids and internal functors, we look at the larger
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-topos"} 2-topos
            of
            %em diffeological stacks
            –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stacks
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
          %p
            Then we can take simply the diffeological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            and consider morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi tra
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tra : P_1(X) \to \mathbf{B}G
          %p
            in the 2-topos. These are now given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctors"} anafunctors
            of internal groupoids, and one finds that they encode a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+cohomology"} Cech cocycle
            for a
            = succeed "-principal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            together with the parallel transport of a connection over it.
          %p This is discussed in more detail at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+theory+--+preparatory+concepts"}> ∞-Chern-Weil theory – preparatory concepts
              \.
          %p
            There is also the diffeological groupoid incarnation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-classes" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X) \to \Pi_1(X)
            that sends a thin-homotopy class of paths to the corresponding full-homotopy class.
          %p
            A parallel transport functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi tra
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tra : P_1(X) \to G
            factors through
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
            precisely if the corresponding connection is
            %em flat
            in that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature+form"} curvature form
            vanishes.
          %h3#in_physics In physics
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            models a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            or more generally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+field"} Yang-Mills field
            or the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} forces
            exerted by such gauge fields on charged particles propagating on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (i.e.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electrons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quarks
            and generally massive particles, respectively) are encoded precisely in the parallel transport assignment of the gauge field connection to their trajectories.
          %p
            More precisely, the exponentiated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for the electron propagating on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in the presence of an electromagnetic field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            is the functional on the space of paths in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi kin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi tra
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\gamma \mapsto \exp(i S_{kin}(\gamma)) \cdot tra_\nabla(\gamma)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the first term is the standard
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+action"} kinetic action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            is a (nontrivial) connection on a trivial bundle, then, as described
            %a{:href => "#Of1Form"} below
            it is encoded by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \Omega^1(X)
            – called the
            %em vector potential
            in physics – and we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi tra
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  tra_\nabla(\gamma) = \exp\big(i \int_{[0,1]} \gamma^* A\big)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations"} Euler-Lagrange equations
            induced by this functional express precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+force"} Lorentz force
            encoded by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            acting on the particle.
          %p
            If instead of looking at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            of the quantum particle charged under a fixed background gauge field look at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            of that gauge field itself, we can use the action functional of particles to
            %em probe
            these background fields and obtain quantum observables for them.
          %p
            This converse assignment where we
            %em fix
            a path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            and regard the parallel transport then as a functional over the space of all connections over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi tra
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi connections
                  %mo →
                  %mi fiber
                  %mo −
                  %mi homomorphisms
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  tra_{(-)}(\gamma) : connections \to fiber-homomorphisms
          %p
            is called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+line"}> Wilson line
              \-observable
            of the theory. Or rather its expectation value in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            weighted by the action functional of the gauge theory is called such, schematically:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi tra
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi D
                  %mo ∇
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi gauge
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi theory
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi tra
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W_\gamma := \langle tra_{(\nabla)}(\gamma)\rangle
                  \=
                  \\int D \nabla \; \exp(i S_{gauge\;theory}(\nabla))
                  tra_\nabla(\gamma)
                  \\,.
          %h2#special_cases Special cases
          %h3#Of1Form Trivial bundle: parallel transport of a 1-form
          %p
            Of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            is a
            %em trivial bundle
            in that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P = X \times G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-algebra+valued+1-form"} Lie-algebra valued 1-form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℊ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \in \Omega^1(X, \mathcal{g})
          %p
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            In terms of this, parallel transport is a solution to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : [0,1] \to X
            we have the pull-back 1-form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi γ
                      %mo *
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma^* A \in \Omega^1([0,1])
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in C^\infty([0,1], G)
            a smooth function with values in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi f
                  %mo +
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d f + \rho(f)_*(\gamma^*A) = 0
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d f : T [0,1] \to T G
            is the differential of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : G \times G \to G
            is the left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            on itself (i.e. just the multiplication on
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r(f)_* : T G \to T G
            its differential and using the defining identification
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi e
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} \simeq T_e G
            we take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r(f)_*(A)
            to be the composite
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi γ
                          %mo *
                        %mi A
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi T
                    %mi G
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                    %mi T
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T [0,1] \stackrel{\gamma^* A}{\to} \mathfrak{g} \hookrightarrow T G \stackrel{r(f)_*}{\to} T G
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+Lie+group"} matrix Lie group
            such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group"} orthogonal group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(n)
            or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+group"} unitary group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(n)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi f
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d f + \gamma^*(A) \cdot f  = 0
                  \\,,
          %p where the dot is matrix multiplication.
          %p
            By general results on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equations"} differential equations
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0)
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The parallel transport of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \Omega^1(X,\mathfrak{g})
              along a path
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : [0,1] \to X
              which we write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi tra
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi P
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %msup
                      %mi γ
                      %mo *
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    tra_A(\gamma) := P \exp(\int_{[0,1]} \gamma^* A) \in G
            %p
              is the value
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(1) \in G
              for the unique solution of the equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d f  + \rho(f)_*(A) = 0
              with initial value
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0) = e
              (the neutral element in
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            The notation here is motivated from the special case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = \mathbb{R}
            is the group of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} \simeq \mathbb{R}
            is abelian, the differential equation above is simply
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d f = \gamma^*(A) \wedge f
          %p
            for a real valued function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in C^\infty([0,1])
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0) = e = 0
            is literally the exponential of the integral of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi tra
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  tra_A(\gamma) = \exp(\int_{[0,1]} \gamma^* A)
                  \\,.
          %p
            In the case of general nonabelian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            this simple exponential formula gives the wrong result. One can see that a slightly better approximation to the correct result is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(\int_{[0,1/2]} \gamma^* A) \cdot \exp(\int_{[1/2,1]} \gamma^* A)
          %p and an even a bit more better approximation by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(\int_{[0,1/3]} \gamma^* A) \cdot \exp(\int_{[1/3,2/3]} \gamma^* A)
                  \\cdot \exp(\int_{[2/3,1]} \gamma^* A)
          %p and so on, with the correct result being the limit of this sequence – if one defines it carefully – as we integrate piecewise over ever smaller pieces of the path.
          %p
            This is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong path-ordered integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            where the above is known as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dyson+formula"} Dyson formula
          %h2#higher_parallel_transport Higher parallel transport
          %p
            The notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            generalizes to that of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+2-bundle"} connection on a 2-bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+3-bundle"} connection on a 3-bundle
            and generally to that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} connection on an ∞-bundle
            %em higher parallel transport
            over manifolds of dimension greater than 1.
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
            for details.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"}> connection on a bundle
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+2-bundle"} connection on a 2-bundle
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+infinity-bundle"} connection on an infinity-bundle
            %li
              %p
                %strong> parallel transport
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+parallel+transport"} super parallel transport
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+Stokes+theorem"} nonabelian Stokes theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy+group"} holonomy group
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+holonomy"} special holonomy
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+transport"} identity transport
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              %p
                (for the relation to parallel transport see
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "transport#RelationToParallelTransport"} there
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p (…)
          %h3#ReferencesInTermsOfParallelTransport In terms of parallel transport
          %p
            There are many equivalent statements of the ordinary definition of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
          %p
            Apparently one of the oldest occurrences of the idea that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            may be reconstructed from its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomies
            around all smooth loops for any fixed base point in the connected base space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            appears in
          %ul
            %li
              S. Kobayashi, Comptes Rendus, 238, 443-444. (1954). (
              %a{:href => "http://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/bpt6k31909/f443.item"}> web
              )
          %p A more detailed and more general discussion has then been given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Milnor"}> John Milnor
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Construction of Universal Bundles, I
              %strong 63
              (1956) 272-284 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1969609"}> doi:10.2307/1969609
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p A detailed discussion of the differentiable case appears is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Costake Teleman
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Généralisation du groupe fondamental
                %strong 3
                77 (1960) 195-234 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=ASENS_1960_3_77_3_195_0"}> numdam:ASENS_1960_3_77_3_195_0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p Costake Teleman, Annali di Matematica, Pura ed Applicata, LXII, 379-412. (1963).
          %p
            In the special case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connections"} flat connections
            (see also
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+systems"} local systems
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"}> Pierre Deligne
              , §I.1 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Equations différentielles à points singuliers réguliers
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 163
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "https://publications.ias.edu/node/355"} publications.ias:355
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p This history is recollected in the introduction of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Barrett"}> John Barrett
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Holonomy and path structures in general relativity and Yang-Mills theory
              %strong 30
              9 (1991) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00671007"}> doi:10.1007/BF00671007
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            who himself gives a proof. Barret implicitly uses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
            structure on the space of loops.
          %p Followups:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Piotr+Hajac"}> Piotr Hajac
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Axiomatic holonomy maps and generalized Yang-Mills moduli space
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00777377"} doi:10.1007/BF00777377
          %p A note on how a 1-form is encoded in the parallel transport that it induces along paths is also in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/hep-th/pdf/9206/9206021v1.pdf#page=56"} appendix B
              of:
              %em Classical Chern-Simons theory, Part I
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9206021"}> arXiv:hep-th/9206021
              )
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              Jerzy Lewandowski
              = succeed "," do
                %em Group of loops, holonomy maps, path bundle and pathconnection
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://ej.iop.org/links/r8XqKR99r/8rbzxFDZ2xGeFw29av5vpA/cq930508.pdf"} pdf
          %p the statement of the equivalence is attributed to
          %ul
            %li
              Jeeva Anandan,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Holonomy groups in gavity and gauge fields
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/169409"} spire:169409
          %p
            Therein it is shown that smoothness of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            is a necessary condition for it to come from a smooth bundle with connection. Barrett also shows that this is sufficient.
          %p Lewandowski adds to this a formulation of an equivalence of bundles with connections and the subset of loops around which the corresponding parallel transport is trivial.
          %p Around the same time appeared
          %ul
            %li
              A. Caetano,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roger+Picken"} Roger Picken
              = succeed "," do
                %em An axiomatic definition of holonomy
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://ccdb4fs.kek.jp/cgi-bin/img_index?9405501"} scan
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0129167X94000425"} doi:10.1142/S0129167X94000425
          %p
            that generalizes these ideas from loops to general paths. These authors introduced the idea of sitting instants of paths and noticed that the most elegant way to (re)state the maximal equivalence relation on paths which is respected by parallel transport is in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+homotopy"} thin homotopy
          %p
            Barrett originally had something very similar but slightly different. With Caetano and Picken’s relation, the space of thin homotopy classes of paths in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            becomes an groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{P}_1(X)
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+groupoid"} path groupoid
            – internal to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
          %p
            Discussion in terms of local functorial data that lends itself to generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
            is given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"} Konrad Waldorf
              = succeed "," do
                %em Parallel Transport and Functors
              %strong 4
              (2009) 187-244 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0452"}> arXiv:0705.0452
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.emis.de/journals/JHRS/volumes/2009/n1a10/abstract.htm"} JHRS:n1a10
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://tcms.org.ge/Journals/JHRS/xvolumes/2009/n1a10/v4n1a10hl.pdf"} pdf
          %p A quick proof that bundles with connections are encoded in their parallel transport along paths was noted in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Florin+Dumitrescu"}> Florin Dumitrescu
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Connections and Parallel Transport
              %strong 5
              1 (2010) 171–175 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0903.0121"}> arXiv:0903.0121
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://tcms.org.ge/Journals/JHRS/xvolumes/2010/n1a10/v5n1a10.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            For more see the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
          %p
            A discussion of parallel transport in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            (motivated by a discussion of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"}> Gonzalo Reyes
              ,
              %em General Relativity: Affine connections, parallel transport and sprays
              (
              %a{:href => "https://marieetgonzalo.files.wordpress.com/2009/01/affineconnections.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p A discussion of holonomic equivalence of curves can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Francis Bischoff, Darrick Lee,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Thin Homotopy and the Signature of Piecewise Linear Surfaces
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16657"} arXiv
            %li
              %p
                Tamer Tlas,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the holonomic equivalence of two curves
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.6611"} arXiv
        .revisedby
